## 💡 Production Code Implementation & Patterns

The production‑ready implementation stitches together a clean domain model, resilient service orchestration, and event‑driven messaging. Below is a minimal yet complete Java OOP layer, a Spring REST façade, and a micro‑service wiring that leverages Resilience4j, Axon, and Spring Kafka.

### 1. Domain Layer – LLD

```java
public interface OrderCommand {
    UUID getOrderId();
}

public abstract class AbstractOrder implements OrderCommand {
    protected final UUID orderId;
    protected OrderStatus status;

    protected AbstractOrder(UUID orderId) {
        this.orderId = orderId;
        this.status = OrderStatus.CREATED;
    }

    @Override
    public UUID getOrderId() { return orderId; }
    public OrderStatus getStatus() { return status; }
}

public enum OrderStatus { CREATED, PROCESSING, COMPLETED, CANCELLED }

public class OnlineOrder extends AbstractOrder {
    private final String customerEmail;

    public OnlineOrder(UUID orderId, String customerEmail) {
        super(orderId);
        this.customerEmail = customerEmail;
    }

    public String getCustomerEmail() { return customerEmail; }
}
```

### 2. Service Layer – Resilience & Event Sourcing

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final OrderRepository repo;
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, OrderEvent> kafkaTemplate;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository repo,
                        KafkaTemplate<String, OrderEvent> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.repo = repo;
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "orderService", fallbackMethod = "fallbackCreate")
    @CommandHandler
    public void createOrder(CreateOrderCommand cmd) {
        OnlineOrder order = new OnlineOrder(cmd.getOrderId(), cmd.getCustomerEmail());
        repo.save(order);
        OrderEvent event = new OrderCreatedEvent(order.getOrderId(), order.getCustomerEmail());
        kafkaTemplate.send("order-events", event);
    }

    private void fallbackCreate(CreateOrderCommand cmd, Throwable t) {
        // graceful degradation: log and persist to a dead‑letter queue
        log.warn("Fallback createOrder for {}: {}", cmd.getOrderId(), t.getMessage());
        kafkaTemplate.send("dead-letter-order-events", new OrderFailedEvent(cmd.getOrderId(), t.getMessage()));
    }

    @EventSourcingHandler
    public void on(OrderCreatedEvent event) {
        // update read‑model or trigger downstream processes
        repo.updateStatus(event.getOrderId(), OrderStatus.PROCESSING);
    }
}
```

### 3. REST Facade

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class OrderController {

    private final CommandGateway commandGateway;

    public OrderController(CommandGateway commandGateway) {
        this.commandGateway = commandGateway;
    }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Void> submit(@RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req) {
        CreateOrderCommand cmd = new CreateOrderCommand(UUID.randomUUID(), req.getCustomerEmail());
        commandGateway.send(cmd);
        return ResponseEntity.accepted().build();
    }
}
```

### 4. Kafka Consumer (Read‑Model Updater)

```java
@Component
public class OrderEventListener {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "order-events", groupId = "order-read-model")
    public void handle(OrderEvent event) {
        // simple projection: persist to a read‑only DB
        readModelRepo.upsert(event.getOrderId(), event.getCustomerEmail(), event.getStatus());
    }
}
```

